Subject: [PATCH] imx296: Sony IMX296 global shutter sensor support (J106 / TX2)

Adds a tegracam driver for the Sony IMX296 so CSI port F of the Auvidea J106
can carry an IMX296 global shutter module while ports A-E keep their IMX219s.
L4T R32.7.6 ships no imx296 driver at all, so the sensor is otherwise invisible
to V4L2 and Argus.

Register semantics follow the Sony IMX296LQR-C datasheet; the values match the
upstream mainline driver (drivers/media/i2c/imx296.c) exactly, which is used as
a cross-check. The ~40-register initialisation sequence in imx296_mode_tbls.h is
vendor tuning magic that the datasheet does not document, and is reproduced
verbatim from mainline.

Two J106 specifics shape the driver:

1) No reset gpio, ever.

   All six J106 CSI reset pins are ONE physical line, already released high at
   boot by the j106-camera-reset-release gpio-hog, and the five IMX219s depend
   on it staying high. Unlike the stock tegracam imx219 driver - which makes
   reset-gpios mandatory and had to be patched to tolerate the sharing (see
   0001) - a driver written from scratch is under no such obligation: the
   IMX296 node declares no reset-gpios and this driver never requests, frees or
   drives that line. There is therefore no code path by which bringing port F
   up or down can reset the other five sensors. Per-sensor control uses the
   IMX296's software standby (CTRL00) instead.

2) The input clock is a device tree property, not the shared MCLK.

   The IMX296 accepts only 37.125/54/74.25 MHz INCK, none of which is the
   24 MHz the J106 fans out to all six sensors - but the fitted module has its
   own oscillator, so it does not need the shared clock and its node names no
   mclk. INCKSEL[0..3] and CTRL418C are selected from an "inck-frequency"
   property, which accepts only those three values and defaults to 74.25 MHz
   (the sensor's own power-on default).

   Note HMAX is a line length in units of an internal reference the sensor
   holds at 74.25 MHz REGARDLESS of INCK, so all line/frame timing arithmetic
   is written against that constant. Datasheet cross-check: HMAX 1100 /
   74.25 MHz = 14.815 us per line, and 1 / (VMAX 1118 * 14.815 us) = 60.38 fps
   against the datasheet's stated 60.3 fps for All-pixel scan mode.

Exposes one mode, 1456x1088 RAW10 (Bayer BGGR for the colour LQR part) at
60.3 fps, with gain (GAIN, 0.1 dB steps 0..480), exposure (SHS1, clamped
against the live VMAX rather than a constant) and frame rate (VMAX) controls,
and group hold via CTRL08 REGHOLD.

The sensor is identified before registering: SENSOR_INFO (0x3148) must report
model 296, i.e. (value >> 6) & 0x1ff == 296, and the mono/colour variant is
logged from bit 15. The sensor must be taken out of standby first - that
register reads back zero while standby is asserted.

This patch is independent of 0001-imx219-share-reset-gpio-j106.patch: they
touch disjoint files and can be applied or reverted in either order.
Note on the power-on settle time: all six J106 CSI resets are one line and the
imx219 driver pulses it at boot (patch 0001), so an IMX296 can be probed within
milliseconds of leaving reset. The datasheet's t4 + t5 + t9 (200 us + 21.2 ms +
1.2 ms) wait is therefore load-bearing here - without it the FIRST sensor probed
on each i2c bus fails its first register write with -EREMOTEIO while the second
succeeds. A short retry around that first access backs it up.
---
--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296.c
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx296.c
@@ -0,0 +1,878 @@
